    {
      "id": "v2/5nn4_SC2_A_1017__top2/r02/G1",
      "evidence_sha256": "dbe1903b4a535a7d61d551f4d86b0ffb5dec4d4f49b9b9727eea0cb75146b516",
      "assessment": "R2 G1 correctly separates +0.055876 against its inherited r01, +0.05444 against R0 and a 0.031279 selected-incumbent deficit. But it borrows the incumbent-pair median and quartile pattern to describe the last edit. It says other selected probes remain unexecuted rather than unavailable to this private branch; that confuses privacy with execution status.",
      "decision_update": "[6,6,5,6] is selected for R3 G3; [7,5,5,6] is rejected after synthesis sees the other group’s tick7 loss. This is a useful role separation: private uncertainty is resolved by the council, without exposing current peers prematurely. The added-window hypothesis remains conditional on the shared next parent.",
      "counterexample_or_limitation": "Its no-clipping diagnosis is supported, but comparing latest delivery with mixed incumbent does not isolate dose. The claimed five-fold fall in guidance/native by the final window is wrong (about 2.3-fold); conditional subgroup predictions require the actual comparison, not the incumbent deficit.",
